1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The Panama Canal.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's a marvel of the modern world.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
At 50 miles long, this man-made passage

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
slices through a continent and unites the Atlantic

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and Pacific Oceans.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
But now, a century after its creation,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
the Panama Canal is too small.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
The race to expand the continent

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,000
The race to expand the canal is on.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
With the amount of excavation that we're doing,

11
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:40,000
this is a mega project.

12
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:44,000
We are literally rewriting the book on building block projects.

13
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:47,000
The Panama Canal expansion will be a game changer.

14
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:49,000
I have to see it to believe it.

15
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Well, I actually saw it and I believe it.

16
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
Now, on a special edition of Modern Marvels,

17
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:57,000
the Panama Canal gets supersized.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Every morning, the Panama Canal opens for business.

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:38,000
For over a hundred years, this icon of engineering has operated with little change.

20
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:45,760
Crowds gather to see one of the seven wonders of the modern world.

21
00:01:45,761 --> 00:01:51,280
The giant shortcut connecting the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans is a crossroad of global

22
00:01:51,281 --> 00:01:54,880
trade.

23
00:01:54,881 --> 00:01:57,680
Right now about 3% of world commerce comes through the

24
00:01:57,681 --> 00:01:59,160
Panama Canal.

25
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:03,760
We put between 38 and 42 ships per day, and we operate 24

26
00:02:03,761 --> 00:02:06,860
hours, seven days a week.

27
00:02:06,861 --> 00:02:11,400
The ships carry millions of tons of cargo,

28
00:02:11,401 --> 00:02:17,580
everything from cars to fuel to food.

29
00:02:17,581 --> 00:02:22,180
But as the legendary Panama Canal turns 100 years old,

30
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:23,520
it's facing a crisis.

31
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:35,660
A new breed of giant vessels called Post Panamax

32
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:37,860
is threatening the future of the Panama Canal.

33
00:02:37,861 --> 00:02:51,240
Post-Panamax container vessels are colossal transporters.

34
00:02:51,241 --> 00:02:54,340
They can carry an astonishing 14,000 containers.

35
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:03,220
There's just one gigantic problem.

36
00:03:03,221 --> 00:03:07,300
They are too big to fit through the canal.

37
00:03:07,301 --> 00:03:11,080
And that is placing the canal in jeopardy.

38
00:03:11,081 --> 00:03:13,620
The larger your vessel is, the more efficient it is.

39
00:03:13,621 --> 00:03:16,740
And post-Panamax ships that are the best and most economical

40
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:19,540
cannot go through the existing locks.

41
00:03:19,541 --> 00:03:22,180
As the post-Panamax becomes the new standard

42
00:03:22,181 --> 00:03:26,620
for global shipping, they are bypassing the Panama Canal,

43
00:03:26,621 --> 00:03:30,640
threatening the very livelihood of Panama.

44
00:03:30,641 --> 00:03:32,540
The Panama Canal is a big revenue

45
00:03:32,541 --> 00:03:35,180
provider for the country of Panama.

46
00:03:35,181 --> 00:03:38,360
We generate $2 billion a year.

47
00:03:38,361 --> 00:03:41,360
So it can provide for education, for health, for school,

48
00:03:41,361 --> 00:03:43,360
for all the things that governments need to provide.

49
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,920
If trade moves away from the Panama Canal,

50
00:03:48,921 --> 00:03:51,960
it would be a big blow to our country.

51
00:03:51,961 --> 00:03:53,760
We needed to do something dramatic.

52
00:03:53,761 --> 00:03:55,240
We needed to supersize the canal.

53
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:02,840
To help save the canal, one of the largest construction

54
00:04:02,841 --> 00:04:05,340
projects in history is underway.

55
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:14,880
The creation of a super-sized Anama Canal,

56
00:04:14,881 --> 00:04:18,800
one big enough to last the next 100 years.

57
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:27,900
The plan is to build new locks right next to the original locks.

58
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:29,560
When we were studying this project,

59
00:04:29,561 --> 00:04:32,480
we analyzed how about if we just expand the existing canal.

60
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:33,980
but that's a revenue provider,

61
00:04:33,981 --> 00:04:35,480
so that's why we decided we need to go

62
00:04:35,481 --> 00:04:37,580
with the third set of locks.

63
00:04:37,581 --> 00:04:39,220
At the Pacific entrance,

64
00:04:39,221 --> 00:04:42,660
they must construct a massive three-step lock complex

65
00:04:42,661 --> 00:04:46,060
that will raise a post-Panamac ship up to 90 feet.

66
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:50,940
Next, they must deepen Lake Gatun at the center of the canal

67
00:04:50,941 --> 00:04:54,000
so bigger vessels can safely cross the isthmus of Panama.

68
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:59,080
And finally, on the Atlantic side,

69
00:04:59,081 --> 00:05:01,140
they have to build another set of locks

70
00:05:01,141 --> 00:05:04,900
to get the ships back down to sea level.

71
00:05:04,901 --> 00:05:08,520
The task is monumental, 50 miles of construction

72
00:05:08,521 --> 00:05:12,700
and dredging across a continent.

73
00:05:12,701 --> 00:05:15,100
And it all begins with excavation.

74
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:26,860
On the Pacific side, the team is hard at work,

75
00:05:26,861 --> 00:05:29,440
clearing a path for the new locks.

76
00:05:29,441 --> 00:05:33,820
They must dig a trench a quarter mile wide,

77
00:05:33,821 --> 00:05:38,560
14 stories deep, and a mile long.

78
00:05:38,561 --> 00:05:41,700
Standing in their way, 30 million cubic yards

79
00:05:41,701 --> 00:05:42,960
of dense basalt rock.

80
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:47,860
Yeah, we've got some hard rock here for sure.

81
00:05:47,861 --> 00:05:49,920
So it requires a lot of blasting.

82
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:56,260
The key, particularly with blasting,

83
00:05:56,261 --> 00:05:59,380
is to not excavate too much material.

84
00:05:59,381 --> 00:06:02,200
So the blasts have to be precisely designed

85
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:05,620
so that we're not over-excavating material.

86
00:06:05,621 --> 00:06:07,840
Precision blasting requires boreholes

87
00:06:07,841 --> 00:06:09,340
to be drilled to an exact depth.

88
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,140
A demolition team then moves in,

89
00:06:14,141 --> 00:06:16,600
delicately placing the explosives

90
00:06:16,601 --> 00:06:18,500
and connecting the charges to a lead wire.

91
00:06:18,501 --> 00:06:32,501
Once at a safe distance, the foreman arms the detonator,

92
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:37,920
waiting for the all clear, and turns the switch.

93
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:55,500
With the rock and soil loosened, it's time to dig it up and carry it out.

94
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:09,500
The beast of the excavation site is the Terex.

95
00:07:09,501 --> 00:07:15,500
It can devour 60,000 pounds of rock with every scoop.

96
00:07:15,501 --> 00:07:18,900
with the amount of excavation that we're doing here

97
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:23,080
is the size of the Central Park in New York.

98
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:25,460
This is a mega project.

99
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:28,580
To haul out all this dirt,

100
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,500
the excavator loads up heavy-duty mining trucks.

101
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:37,980
Their oversized wheels help them shoulder tons and tons of rock.

102
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:45,120
I used to call them the Fisher Price, you know,

103
00:07:45,121 --> 00:07:49,520
little trucks, but let me tell you, they move a lot of dirt.

104
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:58,880
When they are finished, the new excavation will accomplish a feat similar in scale to the

105
00:07:58,881 --> 00:08:02,178
building of the original canal.

106
00:08:02,179 --> 00:08:08,560
Over a hundred years ago, the original French canal builders

107
00:08:08,561 --> 00:08:12,320
planned to dig a 50-mile sea-level passage across Panama.

108
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,680
But their plan failed.

109
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:23,960
Over 20,000 workers died trying to build that passage.

110
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:29,800
When America took over the canal, engineers took a different path.

111
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:36,500
Instead of digging a trench across Panama,

112
00:08:36,501 --> 00:08:41,480
they flooded the entire middle of the country, creating Lake Cotun,

113
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,020
and built locks that raised ships to the higher level of the lake.

114
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:54,880
In 1914, surmounting seemingly impossible odds,

115
00:08:55,580 --> 00:08:57,860
the Panama Canal opened for business.

116
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:04,720
Today, on the Atlantic side,

117
00:09:04,721 --> 00:09:08,560
Crews are excavating a mile-long trench for the new locks.

118
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:17,440
The rock here is much softer than the Pacific site,

119
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:20,380
so we don't have to do any blasting here at all.

120
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:24,560
Precision excavators slice through the Atlantic soil like butter,

121
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:30,100
digging almost straight down, creating sheer cliffs in their path.

122
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,700
Some of the steepest cuts that we have here at the Atlantic site

123
00:09:33,701 --> 00:09:37,780
are over 350 feet high, and those are near vertical.

124
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:48,600
But softer rock doesn't mean the job here is any easier.

125
00:09:51,060 --> 00:09:53,540
This region has a seven-month rainy season,

126
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:57,040
and when the rains mix with the Atlantic soils,

127
00:09:57,720 --> 00:09:59,020
things can get messy.

128
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:02,600
If it gets wet, it turns to mush, more or less,

129
00:10:02,601 --> 00:10:04,760
and it's a sloppy soup of material.

130
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:10,300
We've had several machines that have sunk above the axles in the muck.

131
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:16,480
Despite these punishing conditions,

132
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:18,560
crews press on with their mission.

133
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:27,100
To reach their goal, men and machine will dig for nine years.

134
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:31,060
We have an entire army of dump trucks and excavators.

135
00:10:31,061 --> 00:10:33,060
It's a tremendous activity.

136
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:38,060
But their struggle is only a prelude to the big show.

137
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:45,060
Once excavation is finished, massive machines will perform

138
00:10:45,061 --> 00:10:50,060
Herculean tasks to build the largest set of locks on the planet.

139
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:06,180
In Panama, the upgrade to the world's biggest shortcut is underway.

140
00:11:08,680 --> 00:11:12,140
Construction teams are carving a wider passage across the country

141
00:11:12,141 --> 00:11:17,460
to create a supersized Panama Canal.

142
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:25,320
In order to complete their mission,

143
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:29,340
crews must build two lock structures at each side of the country,

144
00:11:29,341 --> 00:11:32,340
one on the Atlantic and one on the Pacific,

145
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:35,560
to raise ships from the ocean to Lake Gatun.

146
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:39,060
But the challenge is monumental.

147
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:43,060
At a mile long and ten stories tall,

148
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:46,220
these are the largest locked structures in the world.

149
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,660
To build this colossus,

150
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:51,820
they turn to one of the most powerful forces on Earth.

151
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:56,360
People.

152
00:11:56,361 --> 00:12:02,260
Right now, we have about 2,500 people working on the site.

153
00:12:02,261 --> 00:12:06,260
At full capacity, you're going to see 4,500 people

154
00:12:06,261 --> 00:12:08,260
working in this place.

155
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:16,260
This army of builders raised the locks one foot at a time.

156
00:12:16,261 --> 00:12:19,260
But working at this scale demands a relentless flow

157
00:12:19,261 --> 00:12:21,260
of raw materials.

158
00:12:21,261 --> 00:12:25,260
To get the job done, workers rely on

159
00:12:25,261 --> 00:12:29,260
workers rely on tower cranes for support.

160
00:12:29,261 --> 00:12:32,460
At this moment, we have 14 towers working

161
00:12:32,461 --> 00:12:36,640
and giving support to different areas.

162
00:12:36,641 --> 00:12:41,580
The tower cranes can lift concrete, steel, anything

163
00:12:41,581 --> 00:12:44,540
that is heavy, to 14-storage high.

164
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:51,520
One of the key elements they move is steel,

165
00:12:51,521 --> 00:12:53,760
the structural core of the lock complex.

166
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:03,900
Right now, we're using about 200,000 tons

167
00:13:03,901 --> 00:13:06,560
of reinforcing steel.

168
00:13:06,561 --> 00:13:09,140
With that amount of steel, we can

169
00:13:09,141 --> 00:13:15,060
build about 18 times the Eiffel Tower in France.

170
00:13:15,061 --> 00:13:17,860
The forest of steel inside the locks

171
00:13:17,861 --> 00:13:20,460
is the skeleton of the complex.

172
00:13:20,461 --> 00:13:25,520
But before the steel can be installed, it must be shaped.

173
00:13:25,521 --> 00:13:28,920
We have a big facility where all the bar is detailed,

174
00:13:28,921 --> 00:13:31,620
cut and bended, transported in trucks,

175
00:13:31,621 --> 00:13:33,280
and then placed at the side.

176
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:40,880
But steel has an enemy, saltwater.

177
00:13:40,881 --> 00:13:43,620
If saltwater comes into contact with steel,

178
00:13:43,621 --> 00:13:46,740
it will corrode and fail, a potential catastrophe.

179
00:13:46,741 --> 00:13:49,400
catastrophe.

180
00:13:49,401 --> 00:13:52,320
That's where concrete comes in.

181
00:13:52,321 --> 00:13:54,880
Concrete is used to wrap the steel skeleton

182
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:58,020
in a watertight protective layer.

183
00:13:58,021 --> 00:14:00,640
I have been in this field for 25 years,

184
00:14:00,641 --> 00:14:04,440
and this is the hardest concrete I have ever worked with.

185
00:14:04,441 --> 00:14:07,180
It's designed to withstand seawater

186
00:14:07,181 --> 00:14:10,200
for a period of 100 years.

187
00:14:10,201 --> 00:14:13,040
To make enough of this 100-year concrete,

188
00:14:13,041 --> 00:14:15,980
the Panama Canal Expansion Project

189
00:14:15,981 --> 00:14:20,400
built a concrete plant right on the job site.

190
00:14:20,401 --> 00:14:21,920
There's twice as much concrete being

191
00:14:21,921 --> 00:14:23,960
placed in the Atlantic and Pacific locks

192
00:14:23,961 --> 00:14:25,740
as in the Hoover Dam.

193
00:14:25,741 --> 00:14:28,640
It's a tremendous volume.

194
00:14:28,641 --> 00:14:32,580
The process begins at the grinding plant.

195
00:14:32,581 --> 00:14:35,220
Trucks dump freshly excavated rock

196
00:14:35,221 --> 00:14:36,220
into the crushing system.

197
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:43,500
Giant tumblers break down the rock into aggregate,

198
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:48,200
the first ingredient in the concrete recipe.

199
00:14:48,201 --> 00:14:52,980
Concrete is part aggregate, sand, cement, and water.

200
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:59,980
In concrete making, temperature is key.

201
00:14:59,981 --> 00:15:04,560
It must be kept in the 50 to 60 degree range.

202
00:15:04,561 --> 00:15:07,280
But in the scorching hot climate of Panama,

203
00:15:07,281 --> 00:15:08,980
they need one secret ingredient.

204
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:12,500
Ice.

205
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:16,000
Ice keeps the concrete at the right temperature

206
00:15:16,001 --> 00:15:20,500
when it's poured, but it can only do its job for so long.

207
00:15:20,501 --> 00:15:23,500
Whenever we leave the plant,

208
00:15:23,501 --> 00:15:26,500
we place the concrete into the dump trucks.

209
00:15:26,501 --> 00:15:30,500
We need to take the concrete into the pouring site.

210
00:15:30,501 --> 00:15:32,500
We only have 22 minutes.

211
00:15:32,501 --> 00:15:34,500
That's where the race starts.

212
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:51,100
To ensure the concrete gets to where it's needed as fast as possible, an army of specialized

213
00:15:51,101 --> 00:15:54,600
concrete pouring machines stands at the ready.

214
00:15:54,601 --> 00:15:57,100
One of the workhorses of the site is the Telebelt.

215
00:15:57,101 --> 00:16:00,100
It looks like a fire truck.

216
00:16:00,101 --> 00:16:06,940
Telebelt is just a big truck with a long conveyor belt that will deliver the concrete into the

217
00:16:06,941 --> 00:16:08,940
pouring area.

218
00:16:08,941 --> 00:16:11,100
A steady supply allows the Telebelt

219
00:16:11,101 --> 00:16:17,380
to dump over 25,000 gallons of concrete per hour,

220
00:16:17,381 --> 00:16:20,340
because each pour requires over half a million gallons

221
00:16:20,341 --> 00:16:22,880
of concrete.

222
00:16:22,881 --> 00:16:25,760
For pours at the very top of the locks,

223
00:16:25,761 --> 00:16:30,160
crews turn to a specialized machine called the Putzmeister.

224
00:16:30,161 --> 00:16:34,120
The Putzmeister is a pump where you can move the arm

225
00:16:34,121 --> 00:16:37,500
to reach the hard places where you pour it.

226
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:45,940
The 200-foot arm is controlled by an operator, guiding the nozzle to exactly the right place.

227
00:16:45,941 --> 00:16:57,940
The critical last step, construction teams sink industrial vibrators into the mix, stirring

228
00:16:57,941 --> 00:17:02,940
out any air bubbles.

229
00:17:02,941 --> 00:17:11,420
Together, the concrete and steel create a super strong lock structure.

230
00:17:11,421 --> 00:17:18,020
And that structure has to be extra tough, because it lies within an earthquake zone.

231
00:17:18,021 --> 00:17:26,840
On the Pacific side, we have faults that are passing within 50 meters of the third

232
00:17:26,841 --> 00:17:27,840
set of locks.

233
00:17:27,841 --> 00:17:32,880
The new locks must withstand a catastrophic 7.1 earthquake.

234
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:39,100
To withstand these intense forces,

235
00:17:39,860 --> 00:17:43,620
engineers designed the new canal to a higher standard than ever before.

236
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:48,660
We've used state-of-the-practice techniques that have been built and developed

237
00:17:48,661 --> 00:17:51,900
specifically for this project, but have general applications throughout the industry.

238
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:56,280
We are literally rewriting the book on how to design large lock projects.

239
00:17:56,281 --> 00:18:03,280
The new lock design raises the bar for the next generation of mega projects.

240
00:18:03,281 --> 00:18:07,280
But their challenges have just begun.

241
00:18:07,281 --> 00:18:12,280
They still must build the world's biggest gates.

242
00:18:15,280 --> 00:18:21,280
After years of hard work, the Panama Canal is showing signs of progress.

243
00:18:21,281 --> 00:18:25,280
Excavation is complete.

244
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:30,620
and the locked walls are nearly finished.

245
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:35,560
But the construction team still has to tackle

246
00:18:35,561 --> 00:18:37,500
the most critical piece of the new expansion,

247
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:39,760
the gates.

248
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:49,920
We have the very large rolling gates.

249
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:52,720
Four are currently on site here at the Atlantic.

250
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:55,320
Twelve more will be coming over the course of the next year.

251
00:18:56,280 --> 00:19:00,280
These gates are like the Swiss Army knives of the system.

252
00:19:00,281 --> 00:19:04,280
They perform so many different important functions for the locks.

253
00:19:04,281 --> 00:19:09,280
They open and close the chambers to allow the passage of the ships.

254
00:19:09,281 --> 00:19:11,280
They must withstand seismic loads.

255
00:19:11,281 --> 00:19:14,280
It's a very active earthquake situation here in Panama.

256
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:21,280
They also must be able to withstand ship impact.

257
00:19:21,281 --> 00:19:31,280
With all these requirements, building the tallest gates in the world is a mega challenge.

258
00:19:31,281 --> 00:19:39,820
Half a world away from Panama, in a town north of Venice, Italy, master technicians

259
00:19:39,821 --> 00:19:42,040
are rising to the challenge.

260
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:47,160
The gates are fabricated in a shipyard because for all intents and purposes, they

261
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:51,120
are designed and built like a large ship.

262
00:19:51,121 --> 00:19:56,320
Workers begin by building the internal superstructure of the gate, starting with

263
00:19:56,321 --> 00:20:00,680
steel I-beams for strength. These open beams are designed to withstand

264
00:20:00,681 --> 00:20:04,120
tremendous water pressure.

265
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:11,120
Workers then weld the outer plating to the superstructure,

266
00:20:11,121 --> 00:20:17,120
creating a single module the size of two large school buses.

267
00:20:17,121 --> 00:20:25,840
The last step, gantry cranes stack modules together to form a single mega gate.

268
00:20:45,086 --> 00:20:49,140
A finished gate stands 100 feet tall and weighs 3,000 tons.

269
00:20:49,141 --> 00:20:53,120
They are heavy, but they cannot be that heavy for the operation.

270
00:20:53,121 --> 00:20:56,520
So in order to move the gates, we need buoyancy.

271
00:20:57,060 --> 00:21:00,060
In the lower part of the gate, you see a closed area.

272
00:21:01,160 --> 00:21:06,360
Like ships, the gates are hollow, with sealed compartments inside, so they float.

273
00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:12,200
This reduces their operating weight in water, so they can be quickly opened and closed.

274
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:18,780
In operation, the real weight of the gates is only 10% of the weight in dry conditions.

275
00:21:18,781 --> 00:21:24,960
Now that these colossal gates are finished,

276
00:21:24,961 --> 00:21:29,080
it's time to move them from Italy to Panama.

277
00:21:29,081 --> 00:21:31,160
Because the gates are so heavy and so unwieldy,

278
00:21:31,161 --> 00:21:34,300
they really can't be lifted conveniently or feasibly

279
00:21:34,301 --> 00:21:35,460
with a crane.

280
00:21:35,461 --> 00:21:38,500
And so they have to be handled with these large multi-wheeled

281
00:21:38,501 --> 00:21:40,740
vehicles that carry them from the bottom

282
00:21:40,741 --> 00:21:41,780
like a large caterpillar.

283
00:21:48,780 --> 00:21:53,980
Their journey begins on the back of a unique vehicle called an SPMT.

284
00:21:53,981 --> 00:21:57,820
SPMT stands for self-propelled modular trailer.

285
00:21:57,821 --> 00:22:05,020
The pieces with the wheels underneath are connected to one engine, and that can carry more than 5,000 tons.

286
00:22:05,940 --> 00:22:08,940
It takes four SPMTs to carry one gate.

287
00:22:10,540 --> 00:22:17,580
Everything is operated by a worker with a joystick control, who walks alongside as the giant gates are taken to a waiting barge.

288
00:22:18,780 --> 00:22:32,780
When the gate is on the barge, we tow the barge to the next port where a big, heavy

289
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:40,220
transport vessel is moored, and there we roll over the gate onto the vessel.

290
00:22:40,221 --> 00:22:44,540
At the port of Trieste, a special vessel is waiting.

291
00:22:44,541 --> 00:22:49,020
There are only five ships in the world capable of transporting the gates.

292
00:22:49,021 --> 00:22:52,180
They're called heavy load semi-submersible vessels.

293
00:22:56,260 --> 00:23:01,220
Just the simple maneuver of getting it on the vessel requires the semi-submersible.

294
00:23:01,221 --> 00:23:07,140
It's able to sink itself to get in underneath the gate itself and then it raises itself

295
00:23:07,141 --> 00:23:10,780
back up to pick up the load of the gate.

296
00:23:10,781 --> 00:23:12,840
Once the four gates are loaded and secured

297
00:23:12,841 --> 00:23:14,720
onto the semi-submersible vessel,

298
00:23:14,721 --> 00:23:17,780
the 6,000-mile journey to Panama can begin.

299
00:23:19,960 --> 00:23:22,220
It's the first time in history

300
00:23:22,221 --> 00:23:25,260
that such big and heavy equipment

301
00:23:25,261 --> 00:23:27,200
has been sailing the Atlantic Sea.

302
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:33,500
If you're transiting the gates across the ocean

303
00:23:33,501 --> 00:23:35,780
and you hit a storm, then the nightmare scenario

304
00:23:35,781 --> 00:23:38,940
is that one or more of the gates could topple into the ocean

305
00:23:38,941 --> 00:23:40,840
or the entire thing could turn over and sink.

306
00:23:45,580 --> 00:23:47,440
Getting the gates across the Atlantic

307
00:23:47,441 --> 00:23:50,440
is a daunting task.

308
00:23:50,441 --> 00:23:53,140
But an even tougher one looms ahead,

309
00:23:53,141 --> 00:23:56,740
placing these giants at the bottom of the Panama Canal.

310
00:24:08,940 --> 00:24:12,940
More than a century after the Panama Canal opened for business,

311
00:24:12,941 --> 00:24:15,940
its mega-upgrade is nearing completion.

312
00:24:15,941 --> 00:24:18,940
In the punishing heat of Panama,

313
00:24:18,941 --> 00:24:23,940
construction teams have been working round the clock for seven years.

314
00:24:25,940 --> 00:24:29,940
The canal locks, the most complicated pieces of the new upgrade,

315
00:24:29,941 --> 00:24:31,940
are taking shape.

316
00:24:31,941 --> 00:24:36,940
But there's still one important part of the puzzle left,

317
00:24:36,941 --> 00:24:39,940
Installing the jumbo-sized lock gates.

318
00:24:42,940 --> 00:24:45,680
After a month at sea, a shipment of gates

319
00:24:45,681 --> 00:24:48,280
has finally arrived from Italy.

320
00:24:48,281 --> 00:24:50,560
Here you can see the first four gates.

321
00:24:50,561 --> 00:24:54,060
We need 16 for our project, eight in Atlantic side,

322
00:24:54,061 --> 00:24:57,160
eight in the Pacific side.

323
00:24:57,161 --> 00:25:01,060
But getting the gates from the ship into the canal zone

324
00:25:01,061 --> 00:25:02,860
poses a new set of challenges.

325
00:25:02,861 --> 00:25:10,040
The 3,000-ton gates are too heavy for the soft Atlantic soil.

326
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:11,520
The solution?

327
00:25:12,040 --> 00:25:14,420
Build a special road for the one-way trip.

328
00:25:15,600 --> 00:25:17,600
It's basically like an airport runway.

329
00:25:19,140 --> 00:25:22,280
It can carry the weight of the gates when they're being installed,

330
00:25:22,420 --> 00:25:26,000
but it is a temporary work, and so it has to be demolished after the fact.

331
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:30,040
Workers move the gate along this mile-long highway,

332
00:25:30,880 --> 00:25:32,180
one inch at a time.

333
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:36,460
After a careful trip, they finally arrive

334
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:38,600
at the lock gate recess.

335
00:25:38,700 --> 00:25:41,940
But maneuvering the gate into its new home

336
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:44,040
will be the trickiest part of the journey.

337
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:49,840
The challenge of installing these gates

338
00:25:49,940 --> 00:25:53,520
into their designated recess is not too dissimilar

339
00:25:53,620 --> 00:25:55,380
from trying to move a couch into your apartment

340
00:25:55,480 --> 00:25:56,720
through a narrow doorway.

341
00:25:56,820 --> 00:26:00,280
I think if you get halfway in, you're home.

342
00:26:00,281 --> 00:26:01,780
way in, you're home.

343
00:26:01,781 --> 00:26:04,880
But it's a very tight tolerance.

344
00:26:04,881 --> 00:26:05,680
Inches.

345
00:26:09,420 --> 00:26:11,660
The team depends on the self-propelled modular

346
00:26:11,661 --> 00:26:16,100
trailer to negotiate the hairpin turn.

347
00:26:16,101 --> 00:26:20,100
Its wheels can turn individually or all in unison.

348
00:26:20,101 --> 00:26:22,900
The tight tolerances and the very close quarters

349
00:26:22,901 --> 00:26:24,040
make it very difficult.

350
00:26:24,041 --> 00:26:26,240
And it has to be done very carefully and very slowly

351
00:26:26,241 --> 00:26:28,640
in order to ensure that there's no damage to the gates

352
00:26:28,641 --> 00:26:31,340
themselves or to the structures that they're being housed in.

353
00:26:36,740 --> 00:26:39,920
Once the gates are settled into their recesses,

354
00:26:39,921 --> 00:26:44,260
it's time to build the drive system that will move them.

355
00:26:44,261 --> 00:26:46,560
There's a large machinery room that sits right behind the

356
00:26:46,561 --> 00:26:49,400
gates, and that's where the motors and the winches and the

357
00:26:49,401 --> 00:26:53,340
cable equipment that move the gates are housed.

358
00:26:53,341 --> 00:26:56,600
Workers install powerful electric motors that will be

359
00:26:56,601 --> 00:26:57,900
the engine of the drive system.

360
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:03,160
Steel rails at the top of the lock walls

361
00:27:03,161 --> 00:27:05,940
will guide the gate in and out of the recess.

362
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:13,040
The drive wagon is lifted onto the rails

363
00:27:13,041 --> 00:27:14,720
and connected to the gate,

364
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:17,380
readying the system for operation.

365
00:27:19,060 --> 00:27:21,980
Rolling gates move in and out of the lock chamber

366
00:27:21,981 --> 00:27:23,540
is a bit like a cable car.

367
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:25,300
It grabs onto the cable

368
00:27:25,301 --> 00:27:27,860
and then moves back and forth in the recess.

369
00:27:29,700 --> 00:27:31,480
As the gate starts to roll,

370
00:27:31,980 --> 00:27:35,320
a wheel at the bottom guides the gate across the lock.

371
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:39,120
But with so many moving parts,

372
00:27:39,480 --> 00:27:42,960
the rolling gates could become the Achilles heel of the canal system.

373
00:27:45,960 --> 00:27:49,640
The original canal has 750-ton swinging gates

374
00:27:49,641 --> 00:27:52,780
that open and close like a pair of doors.

375
00:27:52,781 --> 00:27:56,780
These gates, which have operated for 100 years,

376
00:27:56,781 --> 00:27:58,780
are the canal's weak point.

377
00:27:58,781 --> 00:28:00,780
If the gate gets damaged on the old canal,

378
00:28:00,781 --> 00:28:02,780
they have to basically shut that lane down,

379
00:28:02,781 --> 00:28:05,780
do a complete investigation as to the extent of the damage

380
00:28:05,781 --> 00:28:06,780
of the gate.

381
00:28:06,781 --> 00:28:08,780
If it's more serious, they have to schedule it

382
00:28:08,781 --> 00:28:11,780
to be removed and taken out of service.

383
00:28:11,781 --> 00:28:13,780
To compensate for the gate's limitations,

384
00:28:13,781 --> 00:28:17,780
the original locks are designed for two lanes of traffic.

385
00:28:17,781 --> 00:28:21,780
If one lane closes, the second lane keeps the canal open.

386
00:28:21,781 --> 00:28:24,480
the canal open.

387
00:28:24,580 --> 00:28:28,280
But the new locks are just one lane, no backup.

388
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,020
With only a single lane for transit,

389
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:32,360
if there's any problem with the gate that can't be managed,

390
00:28:32,460 --> 00:28:36,520
then it is down and traffic stops completely.

391
00:28:36,620 --> 00:28:39,500
It's a serious issue.

392
00:28:39,600 --> 00:28:42,260
The challenge is to design a gate system

393
00:28:42,360 --> 00:28:45,640
that is completely reliable.

394
00:28:45,740 --> 00:28:51,780
All the gate design concept is to provide easy maintenance.

395
00:28:51,781 --> 00:28:54,780
There are 12 compartments.

396
00:28:54,781 --> 00:28:55,780
It's like a submarine.

397
00:29:01,780 --> 00:29:06,780
In fact, if a ship is impacting the gate and water is coming

398
00:29:06,781 --> 00:29:10,780
inside the gate, we still will have enough buoyancy to bring

399
00:29:10,781 --> 00:29:12,780
the gate inside the recess.

400
00:29:12,781 --> 00:29:16,780
The giant gate recess doubles as a repair bay

401
00:29:16,781 --> 00:29:18,780
for a damaged gate.

402
00:29:18,781 --> 00:29:20,540
gate.

403
00:29:20,541 --> 00:29:24,880
OK, you see over there like a window in this part of the gate.

404
00:29:24,881 --> 00:29:27,900
Underneath this window, we will have some structure

405
00:29:27,901 --> 00:29:29,800
to support the gate during maintenance.

406
00:29:29,801 --> 00:29:32,740
So the gate will come into the recess,

407
00:29:32,741 --> 00:29:36,380
and it will be standing on these four windows.

408
00:29:36,381 --> 00:29:38,400
Once safely on its supports,

409
00:29:38,401 --> 00:29:44,300
crews can drain the water from the recess and begin repairs.

410
00:29:44,301 --> 00:29:47,280
But even with one gate down, canal operations

411
00:29:47,281 --> 00:29:50,340
will continue without interruption.

412
00:29:50,341 --> 00:29:53,040
That's because the upgraded canal is being fitted

413
00:29:53,041 --> 00:29:55,320
with pairs of gates.

414
00:29:55,321 --> 00:29:58,200
With the redundant gate system with two gates in each lock

415
00:29:58,201 --> 00:30:01,380
head, this allows for continuous operation

416
00:30:01,381 --> 00:30:03,960
with the other gate, while vessel operations

417
00:30:03,961 --> 00:30:06,040
continue unabated.

418
00:30:06,041 --> 00:30:10,000
The paired gate design is remarkably reliable,

419
00:30:10,001 --> 00:30:12,880
built to last for the next 50 years.

420
00:30:12,881 --> 00:30:16,860
It will keep the canal open over 99% of the time.

421
00:30:16,861 --> 00:30:18,260
And then finally, as if there weren't

422
00:30:18,261 --> 00:30:20,200
enough constraints on these gates,

423
00:30:20,201 --> 00:30:21,880
they have to be designed so that when they're

424
00:30:21,881 --> 00:30:24,300
in the closed position, you can drive emergency

425
00:30:24,301 --> 00:30:27,500
and maintenance vehicles across them like a bridge.

426
00:30:27,501 --> 00:30:29,900
The gates are only one part of the lock system

427
00:30:29,901 --> 00:30:33,260
that must be fail safe.

428
00:30:33,261 --> 00:30:36,100
The other piece of the canal that is critical

429
00:30:36,101 --> 00:30:42,100
is the filling and emptying of the locks with water.

430
00:30:46,860 --> 00:30:55,200
The pieces of the new Panama Canal are coming together.

431
00:30:56,340 --> 00:30:58,320
The lock structures are complete,

432
00:30:58,860 --> 00:31:01,800
and the gates are moving into their recesses.

433
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:07,180
But soon, these pieces will have to face

434
00:31:07,181 --> 00:31:09,220
one of the most powerful elements on Earth.

435
00:31:13,060 --> 00:31:13,820
Water.

436
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:19,300
Water drives the whole operation.

437
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:22,920
Without water, the Panama Canal would not be possible.

438
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:29,320
When a ship enters the locks, water from a higher level flows into the lower level of the lock chamber.

439
00:31:30,380 --> 00:31:31,760
This raises the ship.

440
00:31:36,260 --> 00:31:40,040
When the ship gets to the other side, the process reverses.

441
00:31:40,041 --> 00:31:47,040
But with the new canal, engineers will have to take their design to the next level.

442
00:31:47,041 --> 00:31:53,040
Our big challenge with the third set of locks is to move a lot of water extremely fast.

443
00:31:53,041 --> 00:31:58,040
Each lock chamber is long enough to take the entire Empire State Building inside of it.

444
00:31:58,041 --> 00:32:04,040
And yet at the same time, we have to be able to fill it up in 11 minutes.

445
00:32:04,041 --> 00:32:07,040
That is very fast for a lock of this size.

446
00:32:07,041 --> 00:32:10,040
With water filling the lock chamber this fast,

447
00:32:10,041 --> 00:32:14,040
there could be problems, big problems.

448
00:32:14,041 --> 00:32:16,040
These ships are so much larger

449
00:32:16,041 --> 00:32:20,040
that if you have a small variation in the elevation of the water,

450
00:32:20,041 --> 00:32:22,040
the ship could be damaged.

451
00:32:27,040 --> 00:32:32,040
How do you fill a lock fast and do it safely?

452
00:32:32,041 --> 00:32:36,040
The secret lies deep within the bowels of the lock's complex.

453
00:32:36,041 --> 00:32:36,741
complex.

454
00:32:42,180 --> 00:32:45,820
Engineers have designed a network of underground tunnels,

455
00:32:45,821 --> 00:32:49,980
called culverts, to tame the raging waters.

456
00:32:49,981 --> 00:32:52,360
We are inside one of the very large culverts

457
00:32:52,361 --> 00:32:55,160
that take the water to the locks.

458
00:32:55,161 --> 00:32:57,260
What we're doing with the water here

459
00:32:57,261 --> 00:33:00,200
is making sure that as it goes into the lock chamber,

460
00:33:00,201 --> 00:33:01,140
it's very smooth.

461
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:05,960
When they open the valves, water

462
00:33:05,961 --> 00:33:08,160
surges into the culvert system.

463
00:33:08,260 --> 00:33:10,700
As the water actually comes out the valves,

464
00:33:10,800 --> 00:33:12,460
it's almost 40 miles an hour.

465
00:33:12,560 --> 00:33:14,160
It's very fast.

466
00:33:14,260 --> 00:33:15,600
You wouldn't want to be in these culverts

467
00:33:15,700 --> 00:33:18,300
when they open the valves.

468
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:20,600
The enormous size of the main culvert

469
00:33:20,700 --> 00:33:23,920
helps to slow down the speeding water.

470
00:33:24,020 --> 00:33:27,620
But because it's still moving too fast to be safe,

471
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:31,860
engineers built a specially designed hairpin turn.

472
00:33:31,960 --> 00:33:35,060
The water is coming from behind me in this direction.

473
00:33:35,061 --> 00:33:39,460
It will take a turn to the right and then go through this port here,

474
00:33:39,461 --> 00:33:43,060
out into the lock chamber at a nice, slow speed.

475
00:33:46,180 --> 00:33:50,900
The water enters the chamber through 40 ports at the bottom of the lock walls.

476
00:33:50,901 --> 00:33:56,980
40 smaller openings instead of one large one delivers the water slowly and evenly.

477
00:33:56,981 --> 00:34:01,060
These ports are nice and low, well below the underside of ships.

478
00:34:01,061 --> 00:34:03,620
As the water rises up in the locked chamber,

479
00:34:04,200 --> 00:34:07,440
it should be like a mill pond, very smooth at the surface.

480
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,980
With such a large volume of water pouring into the chamber,

481
00:34:14,080 --> 00:34:15,760
controlling the water level is critical.

482
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:19,560
The ship will be raised 25 to 30 feet in the lower chamber.

483
00:34:20,840 --> 00:34:24,080
As soon as it's raised to the same elevation as the middle chamber,

484
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:25,880
the locked gates can then open,

485
00:34:26,340 --> 00:34:29,180
and they have to be almost exactly the same level, within 4 inches,

486
00:34:29,260 --> 00:34:30,540
otherwise the gates cannot open.

487
00:34:31,060 --> 00:34:38,060
To get the water levels to match, engineers had to design a control system, one that surpasses

488
00:34:38,061 --> 00:34:43,060
the original.

489
00:34:43,061 --> 00:34:49,408
When the canal opened in 1914, its water control system was cutting edge.

490
00:34:49,409 --> 00:34:51,060
Analog water gauges

491
00:34:51,061 --> 00:34:56,290
helped canal operators see the water levels in the lock chambers.

492
00:34:56,291 --> 00:34:58,060
Built-in safeties made

493
00:34:58,061 --> 00:35:02,380
Built-in safeties made it impossible to open gates out of sequence.

494
00:35:06,080 --> 00:35:11,380
Today, the new canal is taking the control room into the 21st century.

495
00:35:12,900 --> 00:35:20,020
Right now we are starting to build the infrastructure for the central nervous system of the Panama Canal locks.

496
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:24,700
Workers start by installing the backbone of the electronic system.

497
00:35:24,701 --> 00:35:29,700
Heavy duty tubes packed with fiber optic cables.

498
00:35:29,701 --> 00:35:32,700
We have over a thousand miles of electrical cable,

499
00:35:32,701 --> 00:35:37,700
60,000 terminations, all the elements that will allow us

500
00:35:37,701 --> 00:35:41,700
to control and monitor the Panama Canal locks.

501
00:35:41,701 --> 00:35:47,700
Today, with ships so massive, the new locks have to be fail-safe,

502
00:35:47,701 --> 00:35:50,700
preventing even the most unlikely event.

503
00:35:54,700 --> 00:36:03,740
The new canal's control system alerts operators of any problems, switching to a backup system

504
00:36:03,741 --> 00:36:04,740
if needed.

505
00:36:04,741 --> 00:36:11,060
Everything in this project is duplicated, from the sensors to the wires to the routes,

506
00:36:11,061 --> 00:36:17,720
so you can actually lose one system and you can continue to operate the locks.

507
00:36:17,721 --> 00:36:22,780
When completed, the Panama Canal expansion will have the digital infrastructure of an

508
00:36:22,781 --> 00:36:23,580
entire city.

509
00:36:26,920 --> 00:36:31,080
All of this is coming together to create a colossal shortcut

510
00:36:31,081 --> 00:36:32,280
that will change the world.

511
00:36:35,620 --> 00:36:39,660
But there is still one big hurdle left to overcome,

512
00:36:39,661 --> 00:36:42,900
one that could jeopardize the entire canal operation.

513
00:36:52,780 --> 00:37:03,600
A new chapter of the Panama Canal is about to begin with the opening of the new locks.

514
00:37:03,601 --> 00:37:09,340
Getting to this moment required the skill and determination of thousands of workers.

515
00:37:09,341 --> 00:37:19,060
Together they've moved mountains, poured miles of concrete, and assembled components from

516
00:37:19,061 --> 00:37:22,740
across the globe.

517
00:37:22,741 --> 00:37:27,180
But engineers still have to overcome one final hurdle,

518
00:37:27,181 --> 00:37:30,540
how the new canal uses the water from Lake Katoon.

519
00:37:36,420 --> 00:37:39,060
At the mouth of the lock complex,

520
00:37:39,061 --> 00:37:43,800
intakes suck in 120 million gallons of water with each passage.

521
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:50,260
The third set of locks requires a tremendous amount of water.

522
00:37:50,261 --> 00:37:52,880
Moving that water is a major design challenge.

523
00:37:54,480 --> 00:37:56,740
A network of underground passages

524
00:37:56,741 --> 00:38:00,460
channel the flow of water between the lake and the locked chamber.

525
00:38:01,920 --> 00:38:05,060
But if you thought pumps were pushing water through these tunnels,

526
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:06,240
think again.

527
00:38:07,060 --> 00:38:11,800
The entire system of the new canal relies on gravity alone.

528
00:38:14,800 --> 00:38:18,040
Two-story-high gate valves are the heart of the gravity system.

529
00:38:18,041 --> 00:38:22,040
When a valve opens, water moves from the higher level

530
00:38:22,041 --> 00:38:24,040
to the lower level of the lock.

531
00:38:24,041 --> 00:38:27,040
It's really amazing that we're moving approximately

532
00:38:27,041 --> 00:38:30,040
120 million gallons through the lock chamber in 10 minutes.

533
00:38:30,041 --> 00:38:34,040
It's all done just by opening gates and closing gates.

534
00:38:34,041 --> 00:38:39,040
But with the new locks, there is a big challenge.

535
00:38:39,041 --> 00:38:42,040
With so much water swallowed up with each passage,

536
00:38:42,041 --> 00:38:46,040
they will empty Lake Cotun, the sole source of fresh water

537
00:38:46,041 --> 00:38:48,480
of fresh water for Panama's largest city.

538
00:38:52,080 --> 00:38:55,080
To solve this problem, engineers will have to cut

539
00:38:55,081 --> 00:38:57,520
the LOX water use by 60%.

540
00:38:59,660 --> 00:39:02,720
This is the Atlantic LOX upper chamber water saving basin

541
00:39:02,721 --> 00:39:03,500
you see behind me.

542
00:39:04,800 --> 00:39:08,100
It's the area that will allow for the 60% savings of water

543
00:39:08,101 --> 00:39:09,260
for the project itself.

544
00:39:13,540 --> 00:39:15,900
Water saving basins are a new approach

545
00:39:15,901 --> 00:39:18,400
to canal design.

546
00:39:18,401 --> 00:39:20,540
They are essentially giant reservoirs

547
00:39:20,541 --> 00:39:24,440
that collect and reuse water in the locking process.

548
00:39:24,441 --> 00:39:27,780
And how the water gets moved from lock to basin

549
00:39:27,781 --> 00:39:31,280
is truly a marvel of hydraulic engineering.

550
00:39:31,281 --> 00:39:33,360
With the water saving basin, we're

551
00:39:33,361 --> 00:39:36,180
able to transfer that water to these chambers.

552
00:39:36,181 --> 00:39:38,420
And that water is saved and filled back up

553
00:39:38,421 --> 00:39:41,060
into the chamber for future use for the next ship.

554
00:39:41,061 --> 00:39:43,700
So we actually can save 60% of that water that would normally

555
00:39:43,701 --> 00:39:46,280
be lost by just reusing it and back and forth

556
00:39:46,281 --> 00:39:49,000
through the locked chamber.

557
00:39:49,001 --> 00:39:51,860
With the addition of the water-saving basins,

558
00:39:51,861 --> 00:39:53,860
the new canal will consume less water

559
00:39:53,861 --> 00:39:57,800
than the existing canal, even though it is three times

560
00:39:57,801 --> 00:40:01,640
larger, making it one of the great achievements

561
00:40:01,641 --> 00:40:03,980
of this supersized upgrade.

562
00:40:03,981 --> 00:40:05,380
You know, this is just one example

563
00:40:05,381 --> 00:40:07,180
of how we've made something bigger than it's ever

564
00:40:07,181 --> 00:40:08,460
been done before.

565
00:40:08,461 --> 00:40:11,460
We've done this on the locked walls, the gates.

566
00:40:11,461 --> 00:40:14,360
This whole project's been a series of biggest and best,

567
00:40:14,361 --> 00:40:15,940
and it sets a standard for going forward

568
00:40:15,941 --> 00:40:17,200
on lots of different other projects.

569
00:40:23,080 --> 00:40:25,480
And if everything goes as planned,

570
00:40:25,481 --> 00:40:31,180
the Panama Canal expansion will transform global commerce.

571
00:40:31,181 --> 00:40:33,040
I think it's going to be great for everyone,

572
00:40:33,041 --> 00:40:36,680
not just for the Panamanians, because by expanding our canal,

573
00:40:36,681 --> 00:40:39,320
we are solving a problem to the whole world.

574
00:40:41,460 --> 00:40:48,640
Today, container ships that are significantly larger go to the West Coast and they use rail

575
00:40:48,641 --> 00:40:51,220
to go to the East Coast.

576
00:40:51,221 --> 00:40:53,600
That's a cost issue, it's a time issue.

577
00:40:53,601 --> 00:40:57,840
Once the canal is done and the container fleet can now transfer through to the Atlantic

578
00:40:57,841 --> 00:41:05,100
side, you're going to change world trade.

579
00:41:05,101 --> 00:41:09,940
The effects of the new set of locks will ripple across America.

580
00:41:09,941 --> 00:41:14,340
in Miami, Virginia, Baltimore, and New York

581
00:41:14,341 --> 00:41:17,540
are all modifying their facilities for these larger ships.

582
00:41:20,160 --> 00:41:23,640
Panama Canal expansion will be a game changer for global commerce.

583
00:41:24,300 --> 00:41:27,500
Some people will try to take as much advantage as they can.

584
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:29,220
Some people will feel threatened by it.

585
00:41:29,840 --> 00:41:32,680
But everyone will have to rethink their entire business model

586
00:41:32,681 --> 00:41:34,500
once this project goes into operation.

587
00:41:36,260 --> 00:41:38,760
So you never know what the market is going to do in the future.

588
00:41:38,761 --> 00:41:41,060
The logs are supposed to last 100 years,

589
00:41:41,061 --> 00:41:44,400
but we also left some real estate for the fourth set of logs

590
00:41:44,401 --> 00:41:46,400
if we ever have to build one.

591
00:41:46,401 --> 00:41:48,400
Over 100 years ago,

592
00:41:48,401 --> 00:41:53,900
the opening of the Panama Canal made the world a smaller place.

593
00:41:53,901 --> 00:41:59,820
Today, the new supersized canal is doing the same.

594
00:42:03,820 --> 00:42:06,720
The giant shortcut between two oceans

595
00:42:06,721 --> 00:42:09,420
remains a powerful symbol of human achievement.

596
00:42:12,220 --> 00:42:14,380
And when the water begins to flow,

597
00:42:15,500 --> 00:42:19,440
a new modern marvel will take its rightful place in history.

